        {
          "label": "Review3.13 research framing: new sibling paragraph after first research overview",
          "old": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"865b52e6\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":true,\"subHeadingColor\":\"\",\"headSpace\":8,\"subHeadSpace\":0,\"blockTopPadding\":0,\"blockRightPadding\":420,\"blockBottomPadding\":0,\"blockLeftPadding\":0,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-865b52e6\"><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What Is Chair Massage?</h2><p class=\"uagb-desc-text\">Chair massage is a short massage you receive sitting up, fully clothed, in a padded portable chair that supports your chest, arms and head as you lean forward. I work through your clothing without oil, mostly on the back, shoulders, neck, arms and hands. A session usually lasts 10 to 20 minutes. The chair folds up and fits in a quiet corner, which is why chair massage is what I bring to employee appreciation days and workplace events.<br><br>Most research on chair massage comes from small studies in workplaces and hospitals, usually with 15-minute sessions. In 52 employees, a 15-minute chair massage at work was followed by lower blood pressure, although the study had no comparison group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9132704/\">[1]</a> In 38 nurses offered a weekly 15-minute chair massage for 10 weeks, perceived-stress scores were lower at the end.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059434/\">[2]</a> In a randomized trial of 60 women on oncology nursing teams, 15-minute chair massages twice a week for three weeks were followed by lower stress scores, and less interference from pain in daily life, than no massage.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34484493/\">[3]</a> Studies of office workers given short chair massages over a month report less neck and back discomfort,<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21688984/\">[4]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28157084/\">[5]</a> and one found a higher tolerance for pressure on tender muscle points.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28157084/\">[5]</a> These studies are small and short. I summarize them, including the weaker results, in the research section below.<br><br>Chair massage is hands-on work by a licensed massage therapist, adapted to you as you give feedback. It is not a mechanical massage chair, and studies of those devices are not included here.</p></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->",
          "new": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"865b52e6\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":true,\"subHeadingColor\":\"\",\"headSpace\":8,\"subHeadSpace\":0,\"blockTopPadding\":0,\"blockRightPadding\":420,\"blockBottomPadding\":0,\"blockLeftPadding\":0,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":0,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-865b52e6\"><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What Is Chair Massage?</h2><p class=\"uagb-desc-text\">Chair massage is a short massage you receive sitting up, fully clothed, in a padded portable chair that supports your chest, arms and head as you lean forward. I work through your clothing without oil, mostly on the back, shoulders, neck, arms and hands. A session usually lasts 10 to 20 minutes. The chair folds up and fits in a quiet corner, which is why chair massage is what I bring to employee appreciation days and workplace events.<br><br>Most research on chair massage comes from small studies in workplaces and hospitals, usually with 15-minute sessions. In 52 employees, a 15-minute chair massage at work was followed by lower blood pressure, although the study had no comparison group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9132704/\">[1]</a> In 38 nurses offered a weekly 15-minute chair massage for 10 weeks, perceived-stress scores were lower at the end.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059434/\">[2]</a> In a randomized trial of 60 women on oncology nursing teams, 15-minute chair massages twice a week for three weeks were followed by lower stress scores, and less interference from pain in daily life, than no massage.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34484493/\">[3]</a> Studies of office workers given short chair massages over a month report less neck and back discomfort,<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21688984/\">[4]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28157084/\">[5]</a> and one found a higher tolerance for pressure on tender muscle points.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28157084/\">[5]</a> These studies are small and short. I summarize them, including the weaker results, in the research section below.<br><br>Chair massage is hands-on work by a licensed massage therapist, adapted to you as you give feedback. It is not a mechanical massage chair, and studies of those devices are not included here.</p></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are general findings, not medical advice or a promise of any result; the full summaries, including the weaker results, are in the research section below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
