        {
          "label": "Review3.13 research framing: new sibling paragraph after first research overview",
          "old": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"d1bf4422\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":true,\"subHeadingColor\":\"\",\"headSpace\":8,\"subHeadSpace\":0} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-d1bf4422\"><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What Is Deep Tissue Massage?</h2><p class=\"uagb-desc-text\">Deep tissue massage uses slow, deliberate pressure on muscles that stay tight. I begin by asking about your goals and how much pressure you like, then check in as I work. Deep pressure is never required: you can ask me to lighten up at any time, and I will.<br><br>Research on deep tissue massage is limited and mixed. In a trial of 80 adults with neck pain linked to tender spots in the muscles, adding deep tissue massage to conventional rehabilitation was followed by more improvement in pain, quality-of-life scores and most neck movements than rehabilitation alone.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36645811/\">[1]</a> A larger trial of 619 adults with ongoing neck pain found that deep tissue massage led to more improvement in pain than advice to stay active in the short term but not by 12 months, and a review of the research found that studies differ too much in what they call deep tissue massage and how they do it to draw clear conclusions.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29861215/\">[3]</a> People given massage, exercise or both in that larger trial were also more likely than those given advice to say they had recovered, at every follow-up through one year.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a></p></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->",
          "new": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"d1bf4422\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":true,\"subHeadingColor\":\"\",\"headSpace\":8,\"subHeadSpace\":0} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-d1bf4422\"><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What Is Deep Tissue Massage?</h2><p class=\"uagb-desc-text\">Deep tissue massage uses slow, deliberate pressure on muscles that stay tight. I begin by asking about your goals and how much pressure you like, then check in as I work. Deep pressure is never required: you can ask me to lighten up at any time, and I will.<br><br>Research on deep tissue massage is limited and mixed. In a trial of 80 adults with neck pain linked to tender spots in the muscles, adding deep tissue massage to conventional rehabilitation was followed by more improvement in pain, quality-of-life scores and most neck movements than rehabilitation alone.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36645811/\">[1]</a> A larger trial of 619 adults with ongoing neck pain found that deep tissue massage led to more improvement in pain than advice to stay active in the short term but not by 12 months, and a review of the research found that studies differ too much in what they call deep tissue massage and how they do it to draw clear conclusions.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29861215/\">[3]</a> People given massage, exercise or both in that larger trial were also more likely than those given advice to say they had recovered, at every follow-up through one year.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a></p></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are general findings, not medical advice or a promise of any result; the full summaries, including the weaker results, are in the research section below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
          "count": 1
        },
        {
          "label": "Ernst 2003: adverse effects and serious effects are distinct",
          "old": "A 2003 review of published case reports likewise found that most serious events followed unusual techniques or massage by laypeople rather than by massage therapists, and concluded that serious events are probably true rarities.",
          "new": "A 2003 review of 16 case reports and four case series likewise found that most adverse effects followed exotic techniques or massage by laypeople, with massage therapists rarely implicated, that serious effects were mostly tied to techniques other than Swedish massage, and concluded that serious events are probably true rarities.",
          "count": 1
